from common import save
E = [
("What is economics?","Economics studies how people, businesses and governments use limited resources to meet their needs. Microeconomics looks at individuals and firms (prices, demand, supply); macroeconomics looks at the whole economy (GDP, inflation, unemployment).",["define economics","microeconomics","macroeconomics"]),
("What is GDP?","GDP (Gross Domestic Product) is the total value of all goods and services produced in a country in a year. It measures the size of an economy; GDP per capita is GDP divided by the population.",["gross domestic product","define gdp","gdp per capita"]),
("What is inflation?","Inflation is the general rise in prices over time, which reduces what money can buy. In India it's measured mainly with the Consumer Price Index (CPI); the RBI aims to keep it around 4%. Deflation is when prices fall.",["define inflation","what causes inflation","deflation","cpi"]),
("What is demand and supply?","The law of demand: when price rises, people buy less. The law of supply: when price rises, sellers offer more. The market price settles where demand equals supply (the equilibrium).",["law of demand","law of supply","demand and supply","market equilibrium"]),
("What is GST?","GST (Goods and Services Tax) is India's single indirect tax on goods and services, launched on 1 July 2017, replacing many older taxes. Main rates: 5%, 12%, 18% and 28% (some items 0%). It's split into CGST and SGST (within a state) or IGST (between states).",["goods and services tax","gst in india","gst rates","when was gst introduced"]),
("What is income tax?","Income tax is a direct tax on what people and companies earn. In India, you file an Income Tax Return (ITR) each year, usually by 31 July; PAN is the tax identity number.",["what is itr","income tax in india","direct tax","what is pan card"]),
("What is the difference between direct and indirect tax?","A direct tax is paid straight to the government by the person who owes it (income tax, corporate tax). An indirect tax is collected through prices of goods and services (GST, customs duty).",["direct and indirect tax","direct tax vs indirect tax"]),
("What is the repo rate?","The repo rate is the interest rate at which the Reserve Bank of India lends money to banks. Raising it makes loans costlier and cools inflation; cutting it makes borrowing cheaper. The reverse repo rate is what the RBI pays banks for parking money with it.",["repo rate","reverse repo rate","rbi repo rate"]),
("What is a bank?","A bank keeps people's money safe, pays interest on deposits, and lends money for interest. Types: savings account (for individuals), current account (for businesses), fixed deposit (money locked for a time at higher interest) and recurring deposit (monthly savings).",["savings account","current account","types of bank accounts","fixed deposit","recurring deposit"]),
("What is UPI?","UPI (Unified Payments Interface), launched in 2016 by NPCI, lets you send money instantly from your bank account using a mobile number or UPI ID, through apps like Google Pay, PhonePe and Paytm. Never share your UPI PIN, and remember: you enter the PIN only to pay, never to receive.",["unified payments interface","how does upi work","upi pin","what is npci"]),
("What is simple interest vs compound interest?","Simple interest is paid only on the original amount: SI = P × R × T ÷ 100. Compound interest is paid on the amount plus earlier interest, so it grows faster: A = P(1 + R/100)ᵀ. Try: \"compound interest on 10000 at 10% for 2 years\".",["simple vs compound interest","difference between simple and compound interest"]),
("What is the stock market?","The stock market is where shares (small parts of companies) are bought and sold. In India the main exchanges are the BSE (index: Sensex, 30 companies) and the NSE (index: Nifty 50). SEBI regulates the market.",["share market","what is sensex","what is nifty","what is sebi","bse and nse"]),
("What is a mutual fund?","A mutual fund pools money from many investors and a professional manager invests it in shares, bonds or both. A SIP (Systematic Investment Plan) lets you invest a fixed amount every month. Returns aren't guaranteed.",["mutual fund","what is sip","systematic investment plan"]),
("What is a credit score?","A credit score (in India the CIBIL score, 300–900) shows how reliably you repay loans and credit cards. Above 750 is considered good. Paying EMIs and card bills on time improves it.",["cibil score","credit score"]),
("What is an EMI?","An EMI (Equated Monthly Instalment) is the fixed monthly payment for a loan, covering interest and part of the principal. EMI = P × r × (1 + r)ⁿ ÷ ((1 + r)ⁿ − 1), where r is the monthly rate and n the number of months.",["emi formula","what is emi","loan emi"]),
("What is budgeting?","Budgeting is planning how to use your money. A simple rule is 50/30/20: 50% for needs, 30% for wants and 20% for savings. Track spending, build an emergency fund of 3–6 months' expenses, and avoid high-interest debt.",["how to save money","50 30 20 rule","personal finance tips","emergency fund"]),
("What is the Union Budget?","The Union Budget is the Government of India's yearly financial statement, presented by the Finance Minister in Parliament, usually on 1 February, with planned income (taxes) and spending for the year from 1 April.",["union budget","budget of india","financial year in india"]),
("What is fiscal deficit?","Fiscal deficit is when the government spends more than it earns (excluding borrowing) in a year; it's covered by borrowing. It's often shown as a percentage of GDP.",["fiscal deficit","budget deficit"]),
("What is NITI Aayog?","NITI Aayog (National Institution for Transforming India) is the Government of India's policy think tank, set up on 1 January 2015 to replace the Planning Commission. The Prime Minister is its chairperson.",["niti aayog","planning commission"]),
("What is the World Bank and the IMF?","The World Bank lends money for development projects in poorer countries. The IMF (International Monetary Fund) helps keep the world financial system stable and lends to countries in crisis. Both were set up at Bretton Woods in 1944.",["world bank","imf","international monetary fund"]),
("What is a startup?","A startup is a new company built to grow fast with a new product or idea, often funded by angel investors and venture capital. A 'unicorn' is a startup valued at over $1 billion.",["startup","what is a unicorn company","venture capital"]),
("What is digital marketing?","Digital marketing promotes products online: search engine optimisation (SEO), social media marketing, content marketing, email marketing and paid ads (Google Ads, Meta Ads).",["digital marketing","seo","what is seo"]),
("What is profit and loss?","Profit = Selling price − Cost price (when SP > CP). Loss = CP − SP (when CP > SP). Profit % = Profit ÷ CP × 100. Try: \"cost price 500 selling price 600 find profit percentage\".",["profit and loss","what is profit"]),
("What is insurance?","Insurance protects you from big money losses: you pay a small premium regularly, and the insurer pays when something covered happens. Types: life insurance (term plans), health insurance, vehicle insurance and home insurance.",["insurance","types of insurance","term insurance","health insurance"]),
]
